\begin{abstract}
Visual Question Answering on visually rich documents (DocVQA) presents a defining architectural dichotomy in contemporary enterprise artificial intelligence: deploying native end-to-end Vision-Language Models (VLMs) that ingest document page images directly versus utilizing decoupled two-stage pipelines combining Optical Character Recognition (OCR) engines with text-centric Large Language Models (LLMs). While native VLMs retain fine-grained two-dimensional spatial geometries, typographic hierarchies, and non-textual visual tokens (such as tables, diagrams, stamps, and handwritten margin notes), they incur substantial visual token footprints, GPU memory constraints, and susceptibility to parametric hallucination. Conversely, decoupled OCR-LLM pipelines offer lightweight token processing and lower inference costs but suffer from irrecoverable information bottlenecks during spatial serialization, table flattening, and glyph-level recognition failures.

In this paper, we present \textbf{DocMind v2}, an industrial-grade dual-stream multimodal document understanding, verification, and diagnostic architecture. DocMind concurrently executes a 4-bit quantized native vision pipeline (Qwen2.5-VL-7B-Instruct) and an ONNX-accelerated decoupled OCR pipeline (RapidOCR with Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct) within a strict 16\,GB VRAM budget on an NVIDIA RTX A4000 edge workstation. To bridge the operational gap between both modalities, DocMind introduces: (1) an end-to-end multi-page sliding window batching and context chunking workflow; (2) a formalized causal failure taxonomy comprising nine diagnostic categories; (3) an automated claim-level cross-modal verification ledger; (4) a dynamic resolution ``look-closer'' 300\,DPI crop re-reading arbitration module; (5) a counterfactual faithfulness test operating via visual patch masking and OCR token ablation; (6) a calibrated additive trust scoring mechanism; and (7) an LLM-guided unified synthesis engine with deterministic fallbacks.

Rigorous empirical evaluation on the DocVQA benchmark ($N=200$ validation items) demonstrates that the native VLM outperforms the decoupled pipeline by \textbf{+29.78\%} in Average Normalized Levenshtein Similarity (ANLS $0.9057$ vs. $0.6079$) and \textbf{+35.5\%} in Exact Match (EM $85.5\%$ vs. $50.0\%$), achieving overwhelming statistical significance ($p = 1.01 \times 10^{-18}$, McNemar test) while reducing mean inference latency from $3,406.2$\,ms to $1,096.4$\,ms. We reveal that \textbf{25.5\%} of decoupled pipeline failures originate from an absolute OCR answer-coverage ceiling where ground-truth tokens are entirely omitted from OCR text. Furthermore, our calibrated trust scoring achieves an AUROC of \textbf{0.7873} (outperforming raw agreement AUROC of $0.7042$), delivering \textbf{100\% precision} at up to $50\%$ selective prediction coverage. Finally, we provide full system telemetry, extensive ablation experiments, and detailed failure case studies.
\end{abstract}

