\section{Introduction}
\IEEEPARstart{E}{nterprise} documents---including corporate annual reports, financial balance sheets, legal contracts, regulatory filings, and complex engineering schemata---represent the definitive medium through which institutional knowledge is recorded and transmitted \cite{b1, b2}. Effectively querying and reasoning over these heterogeneous artifacts requires Document Visual Question Answering (DocVQA) systems capable of comprehending dense text, complex two-dimensional spatial geometries, nested tabular structures, vector diagrams, and typographic hierarchies \cite{b3}.

For nearly a decade, the predominant architectural paradigm in industrial document processing has relied on \textbf{decoupled two-stage pipelines} \cite{b4}. In these systems, an Optical Character Recognition (OCR) engine first scans the document image to identify text line polygons and predict character transcriptions. Subsequently, an information retrieval module formats these recognized lines into a linear text prompt, which is fed to a general-purpose text Large Language Model (LLM) \cite{b5, b6}. This decoupled methodology boasts clear operational advantages: text token processing is computationally economical, OCR outputs can be cached indefinitely in persistent databases, and high-performance text LLMs benefit from vast general pre-training and long context windows.

However, decoupled pipelines inherently introduce an \textbf{irrecoverable perceptual bottleneck} \cite{b7, b8}. When an OCR engine transcribes a visually rich page, it serializes inherently two-dimensional spatial relationships (such as multi-column layouts, table headers, and side callouts) into a one-dimensional textual string. In doing so, vital structural cues are erased. If an OCR engine fails to recognize low-contrast ink, drops a handwritten margin note, or misreads confusable characters (e.g., substituting digit ``1'' for lowercase ``l'', or ``0'' for ``O''), the downstream LLM cannot recover the missing ground truth. While text LLMs occasionally repair obvious OCR typos via contextual hallucination, this repair mechanism is fundamentally ungrounded and can silently generate confident, incorrect assertions \cite{b9}.

