Recently, the advent of native \textbf{Vision-Language Models (VLMs)}---such as Qwen2.5-VL \cite{b10}, GPT-4V \cite{b11}, and Gemini \cite{b12}---has introduced a transformative alternative. Native VLMs ingest high-resolution document images directly through Vision Transformer (ViT) encoders, projecting visual patches into the semantic embedding space of the autoregressive language decoder. By operating directly on raw pixels, native VLMs preserve two-dimensional geometry, table layouts, fonts, logos, signatures, and graphical illustrations without relying on intermediary text transcription.

Nevertheless, native VLMs introduce acute operational challenges:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Computational Footprint and Resolution Bottlenecks:} High-resolution page rendering produces massive visual token sequences. Encoding multi-page documents frequently exceeds enterprise GPU memory thresholds, necessitating aggressive visual patch downsampling or strict page-windowing limits.
    \item \textbf{Parametric Hallucination and Ungrounded Answers:} Modern VLMs possess vast parametric world knowledge. When faced with degraded, low-resolution visual patches, they may generate plausible answers derived from internal language priors rather than verified visual evidence on the page \cite{b13}.
    \item \textbf{Opacity and Lack of Spatial Explainability:} End-to-end neural attention weights across thousands of visual tokens rarely provide deterministic, auditable evidence for mission-critical compliance audits (e.g., verifying monetary figures in financial filings).
\end{enumerate}

To date, enterprise engineering teams are forced into an unsatisfactory dilemma: deploy decoupled OCR systems that systematically fail on complex layouts and visual artifacts, or deploy black-box native VLMs that consume vast memory and occasionally hallucinate ungrounded numbers.

\subsection{Core Contributions}
In this paper, we resolve this architectural impasse by introducing \textbf{DocMind v2}, an integrated dual-stream multimodal document understanding, verification, and diagnostic framework. DocMind operates concurrently on commodity enterprise edge hardware (an NVIDIA RTX A4000 GPU with 16\,GB VRAM), executing both a 4-bit quantized native VLM stream and an ONNX-accelerated decoupled OCR-LLM stream. Rather than treating these pipelines as mutually exclusive competitors, DocMind leverages their orthogonal inductive biases to establish mutual cross-modal verification, causal failure diagnosis, and calibrated trust estimation.

The core contributions of this work are summarized as follows:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Dual-Stream Edge Architecture:} We design and deploy an end-to-end framework that executes both Qwen2.5-VL-7B-Instruct (NF4 4-bit quantization) and RapidOCR ONNX coupled with Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct within a unified 11.5\,GB static memory footprint, delivering multi-page visual question answering in sub-5-second latency on an NVIDIA RTX A4000.
    \item \textbf{Formalized Causal Failure Taxonomy:} We establish a comprehensive nine-class causal error taxonomy (\texttt{misread}, \texttt{lost}, \texttt{wrong\_value}, \texttt{spacing}, \texttt{missed}, \texttt{repaired}, \texttt{beyond\_ocr}, \texttt{unsupported}, and \texttt{format}) backed by deterministic lexical, geometric, and visual heuristics that diagnose why each pipeline fails on any given query.
    \item \textbf{Cross-Modal Verification and Dynamic Arbitration:} We implement an automated claim ledger that extracts atomic factual claims, classifies cross-modal consensus or conflict, and triggers a localized, dynamic-resolution ``look-closer'' 300\,DPI crop re-reading module when answers diverge.
    \item \textbf{Counterfactual Faithfulness Probing:} We develop a perturbation-based faithfulness verification mechanism that counterfactually removes asserted evidence---masking visual image patches for the VLM and deleting text tokens for the OCR pipeline---to rigorously test whether generated answers truly depend on grounded visual evidence or stem from parametric hallucination.
    \item \textbf{Calibrated Additive Trust Scoring:} We introduce an additive, fully explainable trust scoring function combining cross-modal consensus, spatial IoU overlap, faithfulness survival, look-closer arbitration, and embedded PDF layer verification, achieving an AUROC of $0.7873$ on selective risk-coverage prediction.
    \item \textbf{Unified Synthesis and Empirical Benchmarking:} We deploy an LLM-guided tripartite synthesis engine and evaluate the system across 200 DocVQA validation documents, establishing paired bootstrap confidence intervals, McNemar statistical significance ($p = 1.01 \times 10^{-18}$), and fine-grained categorization across seven document archetypes.
\end{itemize}

