\section{Related Work}

\subsection{Document Visual Question Answering (DocVQA)}
DocVQA was formalized by Mathew \textit{et al.} \cite{b1} to evaluate artificial intelligence systems on understanding visually rich document images. The benchmark spans diverse document collections (e.g., the UCSF Industry Documents Library) featuring varied typographies, tables, forms, signatures, and rubber stamps. Performance in DocVQA is traditionally measured via Exact Match (EM) and Average Normalized Levenshtein Similarity (ANLS) \cite{b2}, the latter assigning soft penalties to minor typographical variations below an edit-distance threshold of $0.5$. Early DocVQA benchmarks established that pure text retrieval models fall significantly short when questions depend on visual layouts, figures, or un-transcribed handwriting.

\subsection{Evolution of Document AI Architectures}
Document processing architectures have evolved through three distinct paradigms:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Grid-based Multimodal Pre-training:} Models such as LayoutLM (v1--v3) \cite{b14, b15, b16}, BROS \cite{b17}, and DocFormer \cite{b7} augmented text embeddings with two-dimensional bounding box coordinates and visual feature maps extracted by CNN/Faster R-CNN backbones. Although effective for token classification, these models remained fundamentally dependent on upstream OCR engines for candidate tokens and struggled with generative, free-form question answering.
    \item \textbf{OCR-Free Generative Document Models:} Donut \cite{b18} and Nougat \cite{b19} pioneered end-to-end OCR-free document parsing using Swin-Transformer visual encoders and BART decoders. While they circumvented OCR transcription dependencies, their generative capacity on complex reasoning was constrained by small decoder capacities and limited pre-training scales.
    \item \textbf{Native Multimodal Foundation Models:} The current state of the art leverages large autoregressive Vision-Language Models, exemplified by Qwen-VL \cite{b20}, Qwen2.5-VL \cite{b10}, LLaVA-NeXT \cite{b21}, and proprietary systems (GPT-4V, Gemini 1.5 Pro). Qwen2.5-VL introduces dynamic resolution vision encoders capable of packing variable-sized document images into continuous 2D spatial token sequences without arbitrary aspect-ratio distortion. This enables precise grounding down to coordinate bounding boxes.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Faithfulness, Hallucination, and Selective Prediction}
A persistent vulnerability in foundation models is hallucination---generating statements that are factually unfaithful to the source input \cite{b9, b13}. In document AI, unfaithful outputs are particularly perilous in legal and financial contexts where single-character errors alter monetary values. Counterfactual testing---evaluating model behavior under feature deletion or substitution---has been widely studied in natural language processing (e.g., LIME \cite{b22}, contrast sets \cite{b23}). However, applying counterfactual perturbation directly to document visual patches and OCR text tokens within an interactive production pipeline remains largely unexplored. Furthermore, selective prediction (abstaining from low-confidence predictions to guarantee high precision on accepted answers) \cite{b24} has traditionally relied on raw softmax probabilities, which are notoriously uncalibrated in deep neural networks \cite{b25}.

