\section{DocMind Dual-Stream System Architecture}

DocMind v2 is designed as a modular, high-throughput enterprise document intelligence system capable of running locally on an edge workstation without external API dependencies. The architecture comprises four core subsystems: (1) Ingestion and Page Routing; (2) Dual Perception Pipelines (Native VLM vs. Decoupled OCR-LLM); (3) Cross-Modal Verification and Diagnostic Engine; and (4) Unified Tripartite Synthesis. An overview of the end-to-end pipeline is illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:architecture}.

\subsection{Document Ingestion and Dynamic Page Routing}
When an incoming document (PDF, TIFF, PNG, or JPEG) is uploaded, DocMind inspects its physical structure using PyMuPDF (Fitz). For PDF documents, the system checks for the presence of an embedded programmatic text layer. If at least $50\%$ of the pages contain extractable embedded text, this layer is cataloged as the \textit{ground-truth reference layer} $\mathcal{R}$. For scanned PDFs and raw raster images, $\mathcal{R}$ is null.

DocMind supports two operational page routing policies:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Exhaustive Reading Mode ($\texttt{READ\_ALL\_PAGES}=\text{true}$):} Both pipelines process every page in the document sequentially using memory-bounded sliding batch windows. This represents the default production mode, ensuring no factual details are omitted due to retrieval bias.
    \item \textbf{Lexical Retrieval Mode ($\texttt{READ\_ALL\_PAGES}=\text{false}$):} For latency-critical queries on ultra-long documents ($>20$ pages), DocMind executes Okapi BM25 ranking across the text corpus (utilizing the embedded PDF text layer or pre-computed OCR text). The top-$K$ pages (where $K = \texttt{VLM\_PAGE\_CAP} = 6$) are dynamically routed to both perception streams.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Stream A: Native Multimodal Vision Pipeline}
Stream A feeds raw rendered page images directly into \textbf{Qwen2.5-VL-7B-Instruct}. Page images are rendered dynamically at $200$\,DPI. To prevent GPU out-of-memory (OOM) faults while maintaining character-level sharpness, image sizes are capped at $\texttt{VLM\_MAX\_PIXELS} = 1,003,520$ pixels (approximately $1280 \times 784$ resolution).

\subsubsection{Dynamic 2D Visual Patch Encoding}
Qwen2.5-VL employs a dynamic-resolution Vision Transformer. Unlike conventional ViTs that resize inputs to fixed square grids (e.g., $224 \times 224$ or $448 \times 448$) and introduce severe geometric distortion on standard A4 aspect ratios ($1 : \sqrt{2}$), the dynamic vision encoder partitions the image into $14 \times 14$ pixel patches. A 2D spatial Positional Embedding is applied directly to preserve the Euclidean relationships of page layout coordinates.

\subsubsection{Multi-Page Sliding Window Batching}
Under 4-bit NormalFloat (NF4) quantization via \texttt{bitsandbytes} \cite{b26}, the Qwen2.5-VL base weights occupy approximately $5.4$\,GB of VRAM. However, multi-head visual cross-attention activations scale quadratically with the sequence length of visual tokens. On an NVIDIA RTX A4000 GPU (16\,GB capacity), placing more than 6 high-resolution page images into a single forward pass causes KV-cache memory allocation to exceed the card limit. 

To resolve this constraint, DocMind partitions multi-page documents into consecutive batches of size $B \le \texttt{VLM\_PAGE\_CAP} = 6$:
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{G} = \{g_1, g_2, \dots, g_M\}, \quad g_m = \{p_{(m-1)B + 1}, \dots, p_{\min(mB, N)}\}
    \label{eq:batches}
\end{equation}
For each batch $g_m$, the VLM generates an intermediate partial response $a_m$. If multiple batches yield non-null candidate answers, an identical Map-Reduce aggregation prompt merges the candidates into a consolidated vision answer $A_{\text{vlm}}$.

\subsection{Stream B: Decoupled OCR-to-Text Pipeline}
Stream B employs a two-stage decoupled pipeline combining \textbf{RapidOCR} (based on the PaddleOCR PP-OCRv4 model family, executed via ONNX Runtime on CPU/CUDA) with \textbf{Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct} (NF4 quantized).

\subsubsection{Multi-Scale OCR Text Extraction}
Each page is rendered at $200$\,DPI and processed through RapidOCR's three-stage pipeline: (1) Differentiable Binarization (DBNet) text detection to identify word/line bounding polygons; (2) Direction classifier to correct rotated text ($90^\circ, 180^\circ, 270^\circ$); and (3) CRNN/SVTR text recognition to predict character sequences with associated confidence scores $c \in [0, 1]$.

For each detected line $k$, RapidOCR yields:
\begin{equation}
    b_k = \{x_0, y_0, x_1, y_1\}, \quad t_k = \text{string}, \quad c_k = \text{confidence}
    \label{eq:ocr_box}
\end{equation}
Extracted lines are sorted top-to-bottom, left-to-right to reconstruct a reading-order text document. All OCR results are persisted in a local SQLite database indexed by document SHA-256 and page number, enabling instant cache retrieval on subsequent questions.

\subsubsection{Text Context Chunking}
To prevent context truncation on long multi-page documents, the concatenated OCR text is partitioned into sequential chunks of at most $\texttt{MAX\_CONTEXT\_CHARS} = 24,000$ characters without splitting mid-sentence or mid-page. Each chunk is provided to Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct with explicit page boundary delimiters ($\texttt{--- Page } p \texttt{ ---}$). Similar to Stream A, partial chunk answers are consolidated via map-reduce aggregation into the final OCR answer $A_{\text{ocr}}$.

