\section{Cross-Modal Verification and Causal Diagnostic Engine}

To bridge the epistemic divergence between the vision model and the text model, DocMind implements an automated lexical and spatial verification engine. The verification process is completely deterministic and operates at the level of atomic factual claims.

\subsection{Claim-Level Verification Ledger}
Given $A_{\text{vlm}}$ and $A_{\text{ocr}}$, DocMind tokenizes and normalizes both answers by removing punctuation, canonicalizing numbers (e.g., ``\$1,200.00'' $\to$ ``1200''), and decomposing multi-sentence answers into atomic factual claims:
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{C}(A) = \{c_1, c_2, \dots, c_k\}
    \label{eq:claims}
\end{equation}

For any pair of claims $(c_i, c_j)$ from opposite streams, DocMind computes the Token F1 score:
\begin{equation}
    \text{F1}(c_i, c_j) = \frac{2 \cdot P \cdot R}{P + R}, \quad P = \frac{|\mathcal{T}(c_i) \cap \mathcal{T}(c_j)|}{|\mathcal{T}(c_i)|}, \quad R = \frac{|\mathcal{T}(c_i) \cap \mathcal{T}(c_j)|}{|\mathcal{T}(c_j)|}
    \label{eq:token_f1}
\end{equation}
In addition, exact numeric entities $\mathcal{N}(c)$ are extracted using regular expression parsing. Claims are classified into four mutually exclusive alignment states:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Agree:} $\text{F1} \ge 0.8$, or one claim textually subsumes the other ($\mathcal{T}(c_i) \subseteq \mathcal{T}(c_j)$), and numeric sets match exactly ($\mathcal{N}(c_i) = \mathcal{N}(c_j)$).
    \item \textbf{Conflict:} Non-empty numeric sets differ ($\mathcal{N}(c_i) \neq \mathcal{N}(c_j)$) while contextual word overlap satisfies $\text{F1}_{\text{context}} \ge 0.3$.
    \item \textbf{Partial:} Contextual word overlap satisfies $0.4 \le \text{F1} < 0.8$ without direct numeric contradiction.
    \item \textbf{Disjoint (Unique):} $\text{F1} < 0.4$, indicating that an assertion exists in one pipeline's output with no corresponding claim in the other.
\end{enumerate}

The overall verification verdict is mapped to $\texttt{consistent}$, $\texttt{partial}$, $\texttt{conflict}$, or $\texttt{disjoint}$.

\subsection{Formalized Causal Failure Taxonomy}
When answers diverge or prove incorrect against the reference text layer, attributing the failure to a precise algorithmic root cause is paramount for enterprise explainability. DocMind establishes a comprehensive causal taxonomy comprising nine mutually exclusive diagnostic classes, summarized in Table~\ref{tab:taxonomy}.

