\subsection{Spatial Evidence Grounding}
For any concise factual answer, DocMind locates the exact physical bounding box coordinates $\mathcal{B} = [x_0, y_0, x_1, y_1]$ where each pipeline derived its prediction (Fig.~\ref{fig:explainability}):
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{OCR Stream Grounding:} Line boxes containing the answer's key tokens are queried directly from the OCR database, ranking candidate boxes by token overlap and OCR confidence scores.
    \item \textbf{VLM Stream Grounding:} Qwen2.5-VL is invoked with coordinate-grounding prompt instructions, querying the model to output the precise 2D normalized bounding box $[x_0, y_0, x_1, y_1] \in [0, 1]^4$ surrounding the visual evidence.
\end{itemize}

DocMind evaluates \textbf{Spatial Agreement} between the two readers by computing the Intersection over Union (IoU) of their evidence boxes:
\begin{equation}
    \text{IoU}(\mathcal{B}_{\text{vlm}}, \mathcal{B}_{\text{ocr}}) = \frac{\text{Area}(\mathcal{B}_{\text{vlm}} \cap \mathcal{B}_{\text{ocr}})}{\text{Area}(\mathcal{B}_{\text{vlm}} \cup \mathcal{B}_{\text{ocr}})}
    \label{eq:iou}
\end{equation}
If $\text{IoU} \ge 0.2$ or one box's center lies within the other, the models are marked as looking at the \textit{same place}. If on the same page but non-overlapping, they are marked as \textit{same page, different place}.

\subsection{Dynamic Resolution ``Look-Closer'' Arbitration}
When the cross-modal verification verdict indicates a \texttt{conflict} or \texttt{partial} disagreement, DocMind triggers an automated visual arbitration routine termed \textbf{Look-Closer}:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Bounding Region Expansion:} The contested bounding box is expanded by a $2\%$ spatial margin, subject to a minimum dimension of $25\%$ page width and $5\%$ page height to preserve surrounding syntactic context.
    \item \textbf{High-Resolution Re-Rendering:} Rather than cropping the downscaled $200$\,DPI working image, PyMuPDF re-renders the target page crop directly from the vector PDF at $300$\,DPI.
    \item \textbf{Lanczos Super-Resolution Upscaling:} If the crop width is below $900$ pixels, it is upscaled using high-order Lanczos filtering to ensure high visual token density.
    \item \textbf{Zero-Shot Re-Transcription:} The zoomed crop is passed to the VLM with an unconstrained transcription prompt: \textit{``Transcribe every word and number in this image crop exactly as printed.''}
    \item \textbf{Disputed Token Arbitration:} Let $\mathcal{D}_{\text{vlm}} = \mathcal{T}(A_{\text{vlm}}) \setminus \mathcal{T}(A_{\text{ocr}})$ and $\mathcal{D}_{\text{ocr}} = \mathcal{T}(A_{\text{ocr}}) \setminus \mathcal{T}(A_{\text{vlm}})$ represent the disputed tokens. If the zoomed re-transcription text contains $\mathcal{D}_{\text{vlm}}$ but lacks $\mathcal{D}_{\text{ocr}}$, arbitration decides in favor of the VLM (and vice versa).
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Counterfactual Faithfulness Probing}
To verify whether an asserted answer genuinely depends on the highlighted evidence rather than parametric hallucination, DocMind executes automated counterfactual ablation:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{VLM Visual Patch Masking:} The evidence bounding box $\mathcal{B}_{\text{vlm}}$ is completely occluded with a solid white mask on the page image. The masked image is re-submitted to Qwen2.5-VL with the identical question.
    \item \textbf{OCR Text Token Ablation:} The text tokens corresponding to $\mathcal{B}_{\text{ocr}}$ are purged from the OCR context string. The ablated text is re-submitted to Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct.
\end{itemize}

An answer is defined as \textbf{Faithfully Grounded} if it fails to survive the ablation test:
\begin{equation}
    \text{Survives}(A_{\text{orig}}, A_{\text{ablated}}) = \mathbb{I}\left( \frac{|\mathcal{T}(A_{\text{orig}}) \cap \mathcal{T}(A_{\text{ablated}})|}{|\mathcal{T}(A_{\text{orig}})|} \ge 0.8 \right)
    \label{eq:faithfulness}
\end{equation}
If $A_{\text{ablated}}$ survives and repeats the original answer despite the physical removal of the source evidence, the model has engaged in \textit{unfaithful parametric hallucination}.

\subsection{Calibrated Additive Trust Scoring}
Rather than relying on uncalibrated neural probability logits, DocMind computes a deterministic, explainable \textbf{Trust Score} $\mathcal{T} \in [0, 1]$ for the recommended answer:
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal{T} = \text{clip}\left( 0.35 + \sum_{k} \Delta_k, \, 0.0, \, 1.0 \right)
    \label{eq:trust}
\end{equation}
where each delta $\Delta_k$ is governed by transparent evidence criteria:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $+0.35$ if cross-modal verification is $\texttt{consistent}$.
    \item $+0.10$ if verification is $\texttt{partial}$; $-0.25$ if $\texttt{conflict}$.
    \item $+0.15$ if both readers look at the \textit{same place} ($\text{IoU} \ge 0.2$).
    \item $-0.10$ if readers point to \textit{different pages}.
    \item $+0.15$ if look-closer arbitration confirms the recommended answer; $-0.25$ if it favors the opponent.
    \item $+0.10$ if counterfactual ablation causes the answer to vanish (proving true causal dependence); $-0.05$ if it survives ablation.
    \item $+[0.25 \cdot \text{Support}_{\text{ref}} - 0.10]$ based on lexical overlap with the embedded PDF reference layer.
\end{itemize}
Trust scores are mapped to discrete confidence tiers: $\textbf{High}$ ($\ge 0.75$), $\textbf{Medium}$ ($[0.50, 0.75)$), and $\textbf{Low}$ ($< 0.50$).

