\section{Unified Tripartite Synthesis}

To maximize utility for operational human analysts, DocMind presents results through a structured tripartite interface that eliminates the burden of manual cross-referencing:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Section 1 (Vision Stream):} Displays $A_{\text{vlm}}$, inspected page numbers, inference latencies, and interactive visual bounding box overlays.
    \item \textbf{Section 2 (OCR Stream):} Displays $A_{\text{ocr}}$, line-level OCR confidence distributions, context utilization statistics, and raw extracted text.
    \item \textbf{Section 3 (Unified Synthesis):} A synthesized consensus analysis that consolidates verified facts, flags discrepancies, and explains diagnostic trade-offs.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Synthesis Prompting and Fallback Mechanisms}
When both streams generate valid responses, Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct is invoked with a synthesis meta-prompt instructed to: (1) state verified consensus facts unequivocally; (2) merge complementary details (e.g., if vision identifies a company logo while OCR extracts an invoice line item); and (3) explicitly contrast conflicting numbers while noting which modality is favored by diagnostic evidence.

If the LLM synthesis is unviable (or operating in lightweight CPU demo mode), DocMind executes a deterministic rule-based synthesis engine:
\begin{itemize}
    \item If consensus holds: Emits a verified fact declaration confirming agreement across both visual pixel analysis and OCR text parsing.
    \item If conflict arises: Formulates bulleted contrast statements detailing each modality's finding, appending the causal diagnostic rationale (e.g., \textit{``The vision model is favored because OCR misread look-alike glyphs in low-contrast print''}).
\end{itemize}

