\section{Qualitative Case Studies and Causal Error Walkthroughs}

To illustrate the physical manifestations of the causal taxonomy, we analyze representative real-world items extracted from our experimental logs.

\subsection{Case 1: Confusable Glyph Misread and Contextual Repair}
\textbf{Document:} Corporate Annual Report (6-page PDF). \\
\textbf{Query:} \textit{``What is the warehouse capacity?''} \\
\textbf{Gold Answer:} \texttt{12000 pallets}.
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{VLM Output:} \textit{``The Pune warehouse has a capacity of 12000 pallets.''} (Correct, grounded at page 1).
    \item \textbf{Raw OCR Output:} \texttt{Pune warehouse capacity: 12oo0 pallets}. (Confusable misread: lower-case ``o'' substituted for ``0'').
    \item \textbf{OCR Pipeline LLM Output:} \textit{``The Pune warehouse has a capacity of 12000 pallets.''}
    \item \textbf{Verification \& Diagnosis:} While the surface text verdict reported $\texttt{consistent}$, DocMind's verification audit flagged that ``12000'' was missing from the underlying OCR tokens. The diagnostic engine correctly labeled this as $\texttt{repaired}$: the text LLM healed the OCR corruption from contextual priors.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Case 2: Graphical Logo Beyond OCR Perception}
\textbf{Document:} Consumer Goods Packaging Sheet (DocVQA Item \#2). \\
\textbf{Query:} \textit{``Which brand does the sub brand Cofitino belong to?''} \\
\textbf{Gold Answer:} \texttt{Candyman}.
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{VLM Output:} \textit{``Candyman''} (Correct).
    \item \textbf{OCR Pipeline Output:} \textit{``Not found in document.''}
    \item \textbf{Diagnosis:} $\texttt{lost}$ on OCR, $\texttt{beyond\_ocr}$ on VLM. The parent brand name ``Candyman'' was rendered inside a stylized candy logo vector graphic. RapidOCR failed to generate text boxes for the stylized artistic font ($\text{Coverage} = 0.0$). The native VLM recognized the visual brand mark directly from image patches.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Case 3: Complex Hierarchy Layout Flattening}
\textbf{Document:} Corporate Organization Schema (DocVQA Item \#5). \\
\textbf{Query:} \textit{``Under which department is Stockroom organized?''} \\
\textbf{Gold Answer:} \texttt{Research Service Department}.
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{VLM Output:} \textit{``Research Service Department''} (Correct).
    \item \textbf{OCR Pipeline Output:} \textit{``Library Stockroom Shop Facilities''} (Incorrect).
    \item \textbf{Diagnosis:} $\texttt{wrong\_value}$. The document contained a tree diagram. In OCR serialization, text boxes across multiple horizontal branches were concatenated into a single line based on vertical $y$-coordinates. The text LLM associated ``Stockroom'' with horizontally adjacent peer nodes rather than the parent header box located vertically above.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Case 4: Uppercase Kerning and Whitespace Collapse}
\textbf{Document:} Regulatory Tax Form (DocVQA Item \#8). \\
\textbf{Query:} \textit{``What is the required filing frequency?''} \\
\textbf{Gold Answer:} \texttt{MONTHLY}.
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{VLM Output:} \textit{``MONTHLY''} (Correct).
    \item \textbf{OCR Pipeline Output:} \textit{``REQUIREDFILINGFREQUENCY | TOEXCEEO...''} (Garbled).
    \item \textbf{Diagnosis:} $\texttt{spacing}$. RapidOCR omitted whitespace tokens across tightly set all-caps print, collapsing words into a monolithic string that prevented the text LLM from parsing field boundaries.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Case 5: Cross-Batch Split Limitation}
\textbf{Document:} Multi-Depot Logistics Report (8 pages). \\
\textbf{Query:} \textit{``What are the capacities of the Pune warehouse and the Nagpur depot?''} \\
\textbf{Ground Truth:} Pune: 12,000 pallets (Page 1); Nagpur: 4,500 pallets (Page 8).
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{VLM Behavior:} Batch 1 (Pages 1--6) correctly extracted Pune (12,000). Batch 2 (Pages 7--8) answered \textit{``Not found''} because the query was framed as a conjunction and the prompt struggled with partial multi-hop retrieval across disjoint batches.
    \item \textbf{Synthesis Resolution:} The OCR pipeline, having chunked the entire text across pages, extracted Nagpur. DocMind's tripartite synthesis identified the discrepancy, combined both partial findings, and presented a consolidated multi-depot summary to the user.
\end{itemize}

