\section{Operational Guidelines for Enterprise Deployment}

Our empirical findings provide actionable architectural principles for enterprise document processing systems:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Default to Native VLMs for Visual and Form Documents:} For documents featuring complex tabular grids, vector schemata, forms, stamps, or handwriting, native VLMs are unambiguously superior. Deploying decoupled OCR pipelines introduces severe information ceilings ($25.5\%$ loss rate in our benchmark).
    \item \textbf{Retain Decoupled OCR as a Verification Oracle:} Rather than discarding OCR entirely, enterprise architectures should utilize OCR as an independent verification check. When a 4-bit VLM and an OCR pipeline agree at identical bounding box locations, the factual veracity of the output reaches $100\%$ precision.
    \item \textbf{Deploy Multi-Page Sliding Windows Under Strict VRAM Caps:} Rather than downsampling document resolutions to cram 20 pages into a single context window (which severely impairs OCR token recognition), practitioners should process documents in high-resolution batches ($B \le 6$) coupled with map-reduce candidate consolidation.
    \item \textbf{Employ Counterfactual Probing for Regulated Audits:} In mission-critical environments where hallucinations carry regulatory liability, automated visual patch masking and text ablation provide rigorous, verifiable proof that an answer is causally grounded in the source artifact.
\end{enumerate}

