\section{Conclusion and Future Work}
In this paper, we introduced \textbf{DocMind v2}, an integrated dual-stream multimodal document understanding and verification framework designed for edge enterprise hardware. By concurrently deploying a 4-bit native Vision-Language Model (Qwen2.5-VL-7B-Instruct) alongside an ONNX-accelerated decoupled OCR pipeline (RapidOCR and Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct) within a single 16\,GB GPU workstation, DocMind resolves the fundamental trade-off between perceptual acuity and verifiable grounding.

Through rigorous empirical evaluation across 200 DocVQA validation documents, we demonstrated that native VLMs deliver statistically significant performance gains (\textbf{+29.78\%} ANLS, $p = 1.01 \times 10^{-18}$) while cutting inference latencies by two-thirds. Furthermore, we showed that DocMind's explainability mechanisms---encompassing a 9-class causal failure taxonomy, dynamic look-closer arbitration, counterfactual faithfulness testing, and calibrated trust scoring---achieve an AUROC of \textbf{0.7873} on selective risk-coverage prediction, guaranteeing \textbf{100\% precision} at up to $50\%$ document automation coverage. Future work will investigate end-to-end active learning fine-tuning and cross-page joint graph reasoning across multi-hundred-page corporate corpora.

